  <front>
    <title>Definition of P2MP PW TLV for Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping Mechanisms</title>
    <seriesInfo name="RFC" value="8339" stream="IETF"/>
    <author fullname="P. Jain" initials="P." surname="Jain" role="editor">
      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">Cisco Systems, Inc.</organization>
      <address/>
    </author>
    <author fullname="S. Boutros" initials="S." surname="Boutros">
      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">VMWare, Inc.</organization>
      <address/>
    </author>
    <author fullname="S. Aldrin" initials="S." surname="Aldrin">
      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">Google Inc.</organization>
      <address/>
    </author>
    <date year="2018" month="March"/>
    <abstract pn="section-abstract">
      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-1">Label Switched Path (LSP) Ping is a widely deployed Operation,
Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) mechanism in MPLS networks.
This document describes a mechanism to verify connectivity of
Point-to-Multipoint (P2MP) Pseudowires (PWs) using LSP Ping.</t>
    </abstract>
